net/sched: sch_cake: cake_mq demand-aware rate split (v1)

Upstream cake_mq splits the global rate equally between the active
sub-instances (rate_bps / num_active_qs). When the TX-queue spread is
uneven, lightly loaded instances leave part of their share unused while
busier ones are capped and drop.

Add an optional water-filling split, off by default:

  0  partage egal amont (defaut)
  1  v1 : une instance non saturee reserve son debit servi + 25 %,
     les saturees se partagent ce qui reste du debit global

Runtime toggle, no rebuild:

  echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/cake_mq/rebalance

Each instance publishes its smoothed served rate and whether it was
shaper-limited during the last interval; the tick winner sums the caps
reserved by the non-saturated instances and hands the remainder to the
saturated ones. Lockless, same READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE pattern as the rest
of the sync block.

Ported from Segalmen's experimental series (his 712,
instrumentation/712-net-sched-sch_cake-mq-demand-rebalance.patch,
Segalmen/cake-mq-patches). Three adaptations to our tree:

 - The measurement half is already ours. Our 999-714 carries his 713
   verbatim (rb_bytes charged at the two cake_advance_shaper sites,
   rb_rate, rb_cap, rb_overlimits, rb_saturated) and his 712 adds
   nothing to the struct or to cake_dequeue, so the port is only the
   split: his helper, his knob, his loop accumulation, his formula.

 - His sampling sits at the head of the sync block and anchors on
   q->last_checked_active. That field has had no writer since our
   999-711 and reads 0, which our 999-714 states in its own comment: his
   guard "if (q->last_checked_active && dt)" would leave r at 0 forever
   and the whole mechanism inert. The port therefore keeps our sampling
   block where it is (after the loop, anchored on the published tick) and
   only consumes its output, the way his loop consumes its peers'.

 - His 712 computes new_rate at a single site. Our 999-714 computes it
   twice: once in the stats block, from active_queues as read BEFORE the
   hysteresis, and once in the application block, from the count after
   it. The stats-block value derives from the count as published one
   tick earlier, so q->rb_cap never equalled the rate applied in the
   same pass: it lagged it by one tick, and the saturation criterion of
   our 999-714 compared against that lagging cap. The port computes it
   once, in the sync block, and both consumers read it: rb_cap now
   equals the rate actually applied at that tick.

   That hoist is ours, not his. His 712 computes and applies new_rate at
   a single site and needs no such move; ours has two consumer sites, and
   pulling the computation up into the sync block leaves the application
   block below without the declaration it used to make for itself. This
   patch therefore does not compile on its own -- it needs 999-722, which
   restores that declaration by reading rb_cap back.

Consequence to be aware of before enabling: with rebalance = 1 the sum
of the local rates is still the global rate, but it is no longer
rate_bps / N per instance, so a single guest flow spread over several
queues can be capped below its former share. That is the intent of the
mechanism, not a side effect.

Observable fact, verifiable without a rebuild: /sys/kernel/debug/cake_mq
gains the "rebalance" knob (0644, default 0). With 0 the split is
bit-identical to our current series.

Not built, not flashed. Local port only.

